  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nAn unusually strong and warm subtropical ridge is persisting over\nthe forecast area. This will bring additional threats for record \nwarmth, and an increased threat for fire danger as we go through\nthe next few days. Meanwhile, a significant shortwave trough is \nforecast by all guidance to track southeast from western Canada \ninto the eastern CONUS in the near term. As this happens, a strong\nsurface cold front will push across the area Friday. Unfortunately,\na very tight pressure gradient that develops in the rapid \ntransition from strong ridge to strong trough will result in windy\nconditions Friday. \n\nGiven there is very little to no rainfall expected with the \nfront, the combination of dry/windy conditions will further\nenhance the fire danger risk, and we will likely need to issue \nfire product headlines. In addition, we will to continue message \nthe limited gradient wind threat through our graphics, and \nmonitor the potential for greater threats and wind related\nheadlines. Otherwise, it is worth noting that cooler temperatures\nmore typical for this time of the year will be arriving.\n\nLooking ahead, it appears the lower heights associated with the\ntrough will be temporary in the progressive upper level pattern,\nso expect ridging to quickly return by early to mid next week with\nsurface temperatures warming back up in response. The potential\nfor getting rainfall is quite low with this pattern. /EC/",
